Well, I really hate shell-escape and I've done it dozens of times. :-)
The problem is not that I don't realize what '!' does or how to get out of the
sub-shell; the problem is that one little
slip from '@' means that your screen disappears, you have to wait for a shell
to start up, you have to type exit/^D (and sometimes % as well, e.g. on Suns),
and then you have to wait for a screen redraw.  Depending on your baud rate
& stuff, this can be real tedious.  Why not use a #-command?  #-s is taken, but
#-e(scape) or #-i(nferior) would be ok.

>
>>I guess it's important for the shell escape to be on a single key,

I think three keys is fast enough - #, e, <RETURN>.  Or the shell escape
could be reassigned to, say, ']' or '|', in other words an out-of-the-way,
heretofore unused, shifted character.  (I know, they wouldn't be out-of-the-way
on all keyboards...)

>> [stuff deleted]
